    "created_at": "2023-08-30T10:37:47Z",
    "updated_at": "2023-08-30T10:37:47Z",
    "body": "Happy to make a PR to update this.\r\n\r\n[The RFC](https://httpwg.org/specs/rfc8297.html#security-considerations) actually stops short of using RECOMMENDED (or even SHOULD, which is another similar IETF RFC term), but think it's clear from 1) the wording that is there, 2) that both Chrome and Safari only accept these over HTTP/2+, and 3) that the major implementers like [Cloudflare](https://developers.cloudflare.com/cache/advanced-configuration/early-hints/#:~:text=Early%20Hints%20is%20currently%20only%20supported%20over%20HTTP/2%20and%20HTTP/3.), [Fastly](https://www.fastly.com/blog/beyond-server-push-experimenting-with-the-103-early-hints-status-code#is-it-safe), and [Apache](https://httpd.apache.org/docs/2.4/mod/mod_http2.html#h2earlyhints) only send over HTTP/2 that it is a recommended practice. Even if [Firefox currently would accept it over HTTP/1.1](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1813035#c6) if the flag is enabled.\r\n\r\nSo shall we just drop this from being a note?",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
